body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Unqualified Civil Date-Time v1 Working Contract [# civil-datetime-section-1]

  Status: normative proposal-stage working contract, 2026-11-16. Publication is
  disabled. This new mutable target establishes no immutable snapshot, release,
  implementation or native capacity claim. Existing profiles and snapshots retain
  their identities, defaults and bytes. Implementations MUST satisfy the closed
  [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/civil-datetime-contract.schema.json | operation schema] and the semantic, resource and deterministic
  failure requirements below; schema validity alone is insufficient.

  ## 1. Domain, capabilities and selection [# civil-datetime-section-2]

  The profile is [$ aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-datetime.v1].

  | Capability | Required operations |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilDateTime.Relations] | [$ equal], [$ compare], [$ relate] |
  | [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilDateTime.Arithmetic] | [$ shift], [$ shiftClock], [$ step], [$ materialize] |
  |~ Independent civil date-time capabilities and required operations

  Each claim is independently complete, with every admitted pairing, unit,
  boundary, metadata and failure case required. Relations-only providers accept
  derived bundles without implementing a local producer. Arithmetic accepts the
  specified numerical amount bundles without claiming numerical arithmetic.
  Availability is host-declared; a logical request cannot enable an operation.
  Combined claims additionally verify actual-result arithmetic-to-relation chains.

  Require explicit selection in [$ input.policy.profile] or [$ input.context.profile].
  Validate both supplied configurations, then let policy override context and
  resolve only the effective identifier. Missing selection is [$ invalid_policy] at
  [$ /input/policy/profile]; a syntactically valid unavailable effective identifier is
  [$ unsupported_profile] at its selected field. An overridden well-formed unavailable
  identifier need not resolve. No existing API default changes.

  All set members are unqualified ISO/proleptic Gregorian civil coordinates in
  years 0001–9999 with ordinary seconds 00–59. Reduced calendar fields and clocks
  retain all compatible coordinates. This is an ordinary civil-coordinate chart,
  not a UTC/atomic/timezone timeline or an elapsed-time assertion. No host timezone,
  locale, current date, resolver or database supplies missing information.
  Standalone clocks, date-only bundles, qualified date-times, leap handling,
  non-ISO calendars, general durations and clock-axis stepping remain outside scope.

  ## 2. Closed reusable bundles [# civil-datetime-section-3]

  ### Authored or materialized date-time [# civil-datetime-section-4]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": { "category": "civilDateTime", "value": "2024-03T09:30" },
    "originDateGranularity": "month",
    "originClockForm": "minute",
    "history": {
      "clamping": "none-declared",
      "approximation": "none-declared"
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  Only [$ temporal] is required on this authored/materialized input. When absent,
  infer each origin independently from its current literal shape and default both
  history statuses to [$ none-declared]. If [$ history] is supplied, require both
  members. Retain valid explicit origins even if they differ from the current form.

  Date portions are exactly [$ YYYY-], [$ YYYY-MM], or [$ YYYY-MM-DD], followed by [$ T].
  Clock portions are [$ HH], [$ HH:MM], or [$ HH:MM:SS] optionally followed by [$ .] and one
  or more ASCII decimal digits. Fields have their stated widths; hours are 00–23,
  minutes/ordinary seconds 00–59. Validate actual Gregorian dates. There is no
  whitespace, [$ 24:00], decimal comma, suffix, offset, [$ Z] or [$ &] in this category.
  These restrictions do not change Core preservation of forms outside the profile.

  An hour/minute denotes a half-open hour/minute on each compatible date; explicit
  seconds denote one coordinate per date, with omitted fraction exactly zero.
  Fractions are exact, not measurement bins. Preserve the complete authored string
  at admission, including fractional trailing zeroes; operations emit separate
  derived canonical coordinates. A reduced year can therefore expand into 365 or
  366 separate points/intervals, never a continuous year-spanning hull.

  ### Exact derived set [# civil-datetime-section-5]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": {
      "category": "civilDateTimeSet",
      "pieces": [
        { "kind": "point", "at": "2024-03-01T09:30:00.1" },
        {
          "kind": "interval",
          "start": "2024-03-02T09:30:00",
          "end": "2024-03-02T09:31:00",
          "includeStart": true,
          "includeEnd": false
        }
      ]
    },
    "originDateGranularity": "undeclared",
    "originClockForm": "undeclared",
    "history": { "clamping": "none-declared", "approximation": "none-declared" }
  }
  ~~~$

  All four bundle fields are required for derived sets. [$ pieces] is nonempty.
  Point/interval fields are exactly those shown for their kind. Booleans here are
  endpoint membership flags, not operation predicate results. An interval requires
  [$ start < end]; a closed zero-width interval is rejected rather than repaired.

  Ordinary derived coordinates are complete [$ YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS] strings with an
  optional nonzero fraction whose last digit is nonzero. Trim fractional trailing
  zeroes and omit a zero fraction in produced coordinates; incoming derived text
  must already satisfy this form. Fixed-width fields retain leading zeroes.
  Chronological comparison is by exact coordinate value: authored [$ .1] and [$ .10]
  denote the same coordinate. A comparator may exploit canonical text ordering
  only after its grammar and normalization have been validated.

  The only exceptional endpoint is the closed object [$ {"boundary":"endOfDomain"}].
  It denotes the finite start-of-year-10000 boundary and is permitted only as an
  interval's [$ end] with [$ includeEnd: false]. It is greater than every ordinary
  coordinate. It cannot be a point, start, included end, infinity or authored
  literal. Thus the last minute of 9999 remains exactly expressible.

  Origins are [$ year]/[$ month]/[$ day]/[$ undeclared] and
  [$ hour]/[$ minute]/[$ second]/[$ undeclared] respectively. They describe declared origin,
  not current extent, fractional scale, uncertainty or selectors. Required origins
  can independently be undeclared; do not infer them from derived endpoints.

  ### Numeric clock-shift amount [# civil-datetime-section-6]

  Reuse these closed numerical bundle forms directly:

  ~~~$ json
  { "number": { "category": "finiteNumber", "value": "0.2" }, "history": "none-declared" }
  ~~~$

  ~~~$ json
  { "number": { "category": "exactNumber", "coefficient": "2", "exponent": "-1" }, "history": "approximation-declared" }
  ~~~$

  Use the existing exact finite-Number grammar and coefficient/exponent
  normalization. No host numeric payload is substituted for text. Finite inputs
  may omit history; derived inputs require it. Coefficients have no leading or
  trailing zeroes except canonical zero [$ 0], which requires exponent [$ 0].
  Exponents are canonical signed integer text. These describe [$ c × 10^e] exactly.
  Non-terminating [$ exactRational] is a recognized deferred domain, not implicitly
  quantized. An explicitly quantized finite result can feed this API with history.

  All supported bundle and descriptor objects are closed. Pass an operation's
  [$ value] bundle into a subsequent call, not its full response envelope. No new
  AEON literal, fraction syntax or standard document annotation is introduced.

  ## 3. Exact canonical sets [# civil-datetime-section-7]

  Order ordinary coordinates by Gregorian date, clock fields and exact fractional
  value; compare fractional digits with virtual zero padding without expanding a
  fixed precision grid. The boundary marker is greatest.

  For internal image candidates, sort by lower coordinate ascending, included lower
  boundary before excluded, upper coordinate descending, then included upper
  boundary before excluded. Treat a point as equal included lower/upper bounds
  for this internal algorithm, while retaining its dedicated transport form.

  Scan sorted candidates and merge when the next lower bound is below the current
  upper bound, or is equal and at least one of those touching bounds is included.
  On equal outer bounds, membership is the logical union of their inclusion flags.
  Remove covered points and pieces as part of this union. Emit each singleton as a
  point and each positive-width component as an interval. A missing boundary point
  is never filled: [$ \\[a,b)] and [$ (b,c\\]] remain separate; supplying [$ {b}] permits union
  to [$ \\[a,c\\]]. Two pieces with a gap remain distinct regardless of its small size.

  Incoming derived arrays must already be in this canonical order with no overlap,
  redundancy or mergeable touching pieces. Reject at the offending piece's lower
  coordinate field ([$ at] or [$ start]); reject a reversed/zero-width interval at [$ end].
  Validate each piece's coordinates and membership flags before cross-piece checks.
  Never silently sort or repair external input. Operation producers normalize their
  own internal candidates and emit the exact union.

  ## 4. Requests and settings [# civil-datetime-section-8]

  Requests have exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]. The generic input vocabulary is
  [$ left], [$ right], [$ value], [$ amount], [$ context], [$ policy]; known-operation arity
  narrows it. Context is an object with only optional nonempty scalar-string
  [$ profile]. Policy is an object with the fields below, closed per operation.

  | Operation | Required operands | Policy fields |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ equal], [$ compare], [$ relate] | [$ left], [$ right] | Optional [$ profile] |
  | [$ shift] | [$ value] | Required [$ unit], [$ count]; optional [$ profile] |
  | [$ shiftClock] | [$ value], [$ amount] | Optional [$ profile] |
  | [$ step] | [$ value] | Required [$ dateGranularity], [$ clockForm], [$ count]; optional [$ profile] |
  | [$ materialize] | [$ value] | Required [$ dateGranularity], [$ clockForm]; optional [$ profile] |
  |~ Civil date-time operation operands and policy fields

  Policy may be omitted only for relation/clock-shift operations with selection
  supplied by context. Context cannot supply counts, units or output selectors.
  Date selectors are [$ year], [$ month], [$ day]; clock selectors are [$ hour], [$ minute],
  [$ second]. Shift units are [$ day], [$ month], [$ year], [$ eom], [$ eoy].

  Count is canonical signed whole-number text: [$ 0], or optional minus followed by a
  nonzero digit and further digits. Native JSON numbers, [$ +1], [$ -0], [$ 01], [$ 1.0]
  and exponential counts are not this portable setting. Count-text guards precede
  conversion; compare large magnitudes against the bounded domain without host
  floating point or unbounded expansion.

  ## 5. Relations and arithmetic [# civil-datetime-section-9]

  All relation outputs are [$ {"category":"symbol","value":"label"}]. AEON notation
  remains e.g. [$ |contains|]; there are no Boolean result predicates.

  - [$ equal]: [$ equal] or [$ notEqual] from exact covered-set equality, independent of
    representation, origin and history.
  - [$ compare]: identical sets give [$ equal]; all left coordinates strictly before
    all right coordinates give [$ before]; reverse gives [$ after]; otherwise
    [$ indeterminate]. At coincident extreme bounds, strict whole-set precedence
    holds if at least one touching bound is excluded. This is not a total order.
  - [$ relate]: [$ equal], strict [$ contains], strict [$ containedBy], [$ overlaps] when
    intersection is nonempty with neither containment, or [$ disjoint]. A shared
    boundary is in the intersection only if both sets include it. Disjoint sets
    may interleave and compare as indeterminate.

  Use endpoint-aware linear sweeps for containment/intersection, not enumeration
  of all coordinates or a Cartesian product of input pieces.

  [$ shift] maps every source date under the existing civil-date rule and preserves
  each clock subset. Month/year shifts preserve day or month/day where possible;
  EOM/EOY preserve calendar-day distance from period end and clamp at target start
  when necessary. Multi-unit shifts compute the direct target and clamp once.
  Clamping can reorder clock images, so map the agreed day fragments and normalize
  all candidates; never map only the original interval's outer endpoints.

  [$ shiftClock] translates the exact complete set by a signed finite-decimal number
  of ordinary civil-coordinate seconds. Preserve gaps and endpoint inclusion while
  carrying across dates. A minute shifted by [$ 0.5] seconds remains a minute-width
  interval starting at that fractional coordinate, not a complete minute tick.
  This does not describe elapsed seconds under a timezone/leap authority.

  [$ step] requires exact coverage of one complete form at both explicit selectors.
  Move the selected calendar period by count units of its own date granularity and
  reconstruct every compatible date with the same exact clock constraint. A month
  step of [$ 2024-02T09:30] yields the complete March form; its pointwise one-month
  shift covers March 1–29 instead. Partial coverage is [$ incompatible_operand].
  There is no clock-axis/joint-grid stepping or restoration of a pre-clamp anchor.

  [$ materialize] requires exact coverage of one selected existing date-time form.
  Otherwise return [$ unrepresentable_result]; keep the separate input bundle usable.
  Emit [$ YYYY-Tclock], [$ YYYY-MMTclock] or [$ YYYY-MM-DDTclock]. Hour emits [$ HH], minute
  emits [$ HH:MM], and second emits [$ HH:MM:SS] plus the canonical nonzero fraction.
  Generated spelling does not reconstruct an earlier authored fractional scale.

  ### Deterministic complete-form candidate [# civil-datetime-section-10]

  Take the first piece's lower coordinate, whether included or excluded. Its date
  selects the one candidate day/month/year at the requested date granularity. Its
  clock fields select the containing hour/minute for those clock forms; second
  uses that exact lower clock coordinate, including its fraction. This merely
  constructs a candidate, not a successful refinement or rounding of the operand.
  Construct the same selected clock constraint on every compatible date. Compare
  the complete candidate extent with the input. No alternative candidate or wider
  containing period is searched. An excluded lower bound necessarily fails an
  otherwise coincident complete-form match because complete forms include it.

  Full date-times in second form are points. Reduced-date second forms are one
  point per compatible date. Thus positive-width second intervals do not
  materialize merely because their endpoints show seconds. Every candidate is
  nonempty and has 1, 28–31, or 365–366 pieces according to its date period.

  Any out-of-domain member rejects the whole transformation. An excluded upper
  boundary exactly at end-of-domain is legal; a point or included upper boundary
  there is not. An excluded bound beyond that boundary still implies out-of-domain
  members. Do not clip, widen, drop subsets or return a partial value.

  ## 6. Metadata and result envelopes [# civil-datetime-section-11]

  Temporal successes have exactly [$ outcome], [$ value], [$ metadata]. Shift/clock-shift/
  step return [$ civilDateTimeSet]; materialization returns [$ civilDateTime], always
  with both origins and both history statuses explicit. Preserve both origins and
  copy incoming histories; additionally:

  - If any source coordinate clamps, set clamping history to [$ clamping-declared].
  - If a clock amount has [$ approximation-declared], set approximation history to
    that status. Otherwise preserve the temporal operand's declared status.
  - Zero/reversing shifts, subsequent exact calculations, stepping and
    materialization never clear either marker. Step/materialize/clock shifts do
    not introduce clamping.

  Metadata has [$ evaluation: "exact-for-policy"], [$ clamping: "applied"] or
  [$ "not-applied"], and [$ effectivePolicy] containing precisely the resolved profile
  and the operation's supplied settings. The clock amount is an operand, not an
  effective-policy field. Exact current evaluation makes no source accuracy claim.

  For example, shifting [$ 2024-03-01T23:59:59.9] by a numerical [$ 0.2] bundle carrying
  [$ approximation-declared] returns:

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "outcome": "value",
    "value": {
      "temporal": {
        "category": "civilDateTimeSet",
        "pieces": [{ "kind": "point", "at": "2024-03-02T00:00:00.1" }]
      },
      "originDateGranularity": "day",
      "originClockForm": "second",
      "history": { "clamping": "none-declared", "approximation": "approximation-declared" }
    },
    "metadata": {
      "evaluation": "exact-for-policy",
      "clamping": "not-applied",
      "effectivePolicy": { "profile": "aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-datetime.v1" }
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  Relations use the same outer envelope and evaluation/clamping/effective-policy
  metadata, plus [$ operandHistory] and [$ operandOrigin], each with explicit [$ left]
  and [$ right] members. Each history member has the two temporal statuses; each
  origin member has [$ dateGranularity] and [$ clockForm]. This preserves ownership
  without manufacturing a new temporal value's history for a symbol.

  History is a value-level declaration, not a point-specific source archive,
  measurement interval, error bound or proof that no earlier approximation occurred.
  Applications exporting only bare date-time text deliberately lose these markers.
  No standard document annotation or automatic export/re-import recovery is added.

  Diagnostics have exactly [$ outcome: "diagnostic"], [$ reason] and
  [$ context: {"stage": n, "path": pointer-or-null}], optionally scalar [$ message].
  They have no value, metadata, partial pieces, automatic null or fallback profile.
  Messages are non-normative. Errors can only create application values through
  separately explicit application error capture.

  ## 7. Portable work and piece accounting [# civil-datetime-section-12]

  P is the admitted canonical temporal input-piece count, L/R the relation operand
  counts, D the sum of nonempty source-day intersections, S the complete source
  candidate's piece count, T the step target's piece count. Counts are logical
  admission units, independent of actual allocation, batching or early answers.

  | Operation | Work units | Intermediate candidates | Final semantic pieces |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | Day shift or any zero-count calendar shift | P | P | Canonical image count |
  | Nonzero month/year/EOM/EOY shift | D | D | Canonical image count |
  | Clock shift, including zero | P | P | P |
  | Relation | L + R | 0 | 0; result is a symbol |
  | Materialize | P + S | S | S, despite compact literal spelling |
  | Step | P + S + T | S + T | T |
  |~ Portable work, intermediate candidates and final pieces by operation

  Input limits apply independently to each temporal operand's exact expansion.
  Intermediate limits count all recipe candidates for this operation, not peak
  live memory; verification templates count even if never physically allocated.
  Final limits apply after exact canonical union, not to an arbitrary unsorted
  prefix. Relation outputs consume no temporal-piece allowance. Input byte/depth,
  fractional/number and result-size limits still apply to every operation.

  Civil days partition the coordinate chart as midnight-inclusive to next-midnight-
  exclusive, with the boundary marker at the last day's end. Count only nonempty
  piece/day intersections. Included midnight can add a singleton on the next day;
  excluded midnight cannot. Several pieces on one day count separately. When
  mapping a fragment, its excluded day end maps to the target day's excluded end,
  not an independently shifted member of the next source day. A source interval
  can therefore produce reordered disconnected clock subsets under clamping.

  Compute D from complete date ordinals and endpoint flags without first listing
  all intersected days. Guard work and intermediate counts before expanding them.
  For stepping, guard P + S and S before checking source-template equality, then
  guard the total P + S + T and S + T before constructing target pieces. A zero
  step pays the full same recipe. Materialization similarly guards its template
  before matching. Source-template budget admission precedes matching. Target-domain checks follow
  a successful source match and precede total target-construction guards.

  ## 8. Exact clock arithmetic and pre-construction guards [# civil-datetime-section-13]

  An implementation may use exact decimal seconds since [$ 0001-01-01T00:00:00]
  internally; this is an algorithm coordinate origin, not a portable instant or
  new wire encoding. Let B = 315537897600 be the finite excluded upper boundary in
  ordinary civil-coordinate seconds. Never use host binary floats or fixed ticks.

  Admit the amount under the existing finite grammar/canonical decimal rules and
  numeric text/coefficient/exponent guards, including authored exponent magnitude
  even for zero. Normalize by bounded text operations, not powers of ten. A huge
  exponent cannot cause expansion before a capacity/domain decision.

  First preflight translation against [$ \\[0,B)] using the input's exact extreme
  bounds and membership flags. Compare the compact amount with the exact permitted
  lower/upper displacement bounds using decimal coefficient lengths, exponents
  and virtual zeroes. Do not expand the amount merely to compare magnitudes.
  Equality at lower zero is legal; equality at upper B is legal only for an
  excluded upper bound. An amount with magnitude at least B cannot translate any
  nonempty admitted set successfully and is rejected without expansion.

  For a domain-admitted nonzero amount, let f be the largest normalized fractional
  digit count of any ordinary input endpoint/point, a = max(0, -e) for the normalized
  amount, and q = max(f,a). Numeric workspace admission is **13 + q decimal digits**:
  12 whole-second digits suffice below B, and one additional digit covers a carry.
  Reject before constructing a scale factor or aligned integers. A zero amount
  needs zero expanded numeric-workspace digits, after all ordinary admission and
  piece-work checks; it does not bypass those checks or result limits.

  All admitted input coordinates have f within the fraction allowance. If a exceeds
  that allowance for a nonzero normalized amount, a generated fractional digit at
  that scale cannot cancel against the coarser input. Reject with [$ resource_limit]
  before expansion. Otherwise q bounds every resulting canonical fraction. Use
  exact aligned integer arithmetic only after workspace/fraction/work guards pass,
  then normalize and render coordinates. Example: [$ 1e-1000000] can be rejected
  without creating a million-digit denominator; [$ 1e1000000] fails domain preflight
  without creating its integer expansion. Neither is rounded into an admissible
  amount. Input numeric limits still take precedence over either operation check.

  Calendar shifts and steps preserve clock fractions and require no decimal
  alignment. Relations compare bounded fractional text without expanding a scale
  factor. Their numeric-workspace charge is zero; their other text/piece/work
  guards still apply. This recipe does not modify any numerical profile's own
  workspace rules or claim its arithmetic capability.

  ## 9. Deterministic validation and diagnostics [# civil-datetime-section-14]

  Stop at the first reached failure. These seven stages extend existing
  contracts with template/workspace guards required before expensive construction.

  | Stage | Ordered checks |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | 1 | Scalar Unicode, actual logical JSON UTF-8 bytes and per-path container depth before deep decoding. [$ invalid_operand] for malformed scalar text; reached byte/depth [$ resource_limit]; null path. |
  | 2 | Strict JSON/duplicate keys; closed request/input structure, operation availability, then operation-specific arity. [$ invalid_operand] or [$ unsupported_operation]. |
  | 3 | Context structure, policy structure/required fields, context profile, policy profile, unit/selectors/count values, effective profile selection/availability. [$ invalid_policy], count-text [$ resource_limit], or [$ unsupported_profile]. |
  | 4 | Supported/deferred category and closed bundle/descriptor/metadata validation. Binary temporal left then right; unary temporal value then clock amount. Input piece, temporal text, fraction and numeric guards are applied before their respective expansions/conversions. [$ invalid_operand] or [$ resource_limit]. |
  | 5 | Recognized deferred domains in operand order: [$ unsupported_domain] at the relevant descriptor's category field. |
  | 6 | Apply the operation recipe below: compact preflight, workspace/work/candidate guards, exact construction/matching and domain checks. Failures are [$ resource_limit], [$ unrepresentable_result], or step's [$ incompatible_operand]. |
  | 7 | Materialization's exact representability, final canonical semantic-piece allowance, materialized text allowance, then successful compact-envelope byte guard before serialization. [$ unrepresentable_result] or [$ resource_limit]. Other successes also pay applicable final-piece/envelope guards. |
  |~ Civil date-time validation stages and ordered checks

  Within a closed object, check required fields in their listed order before
  unknown fields; order unknown decoded keys by Unicode scalar value. Escape JSON
  Pointer tokens normally. Shapes/defaults of overridden configuration are still
  checked. Required count/selectors precede availability of a syntactically valid
  unknown profile. Duplicate object keys fail at stage 2, null path.

  At stage 4 require the descriptor's object/string category first. On supported
  derived temporal bundles require origins then history, reject unknown bundle
  fields, validate history then date origin then clock origin, and validate the
  descriptor required/unknown fields before its payload. Authored bundles use the
  same supplied-metadata order before their defaults. History fields are checked
  clamping then approximation. Piece arrays must be nonempty before their length
  allowance is tested; test that allowance before traversing contents. Validate
  each piece's required/unknown fields, then start/at, end and inclusion flags,
  then coordinate order and cross-piece canonicality. Boundary placement is
  validated with its inclusion flag. Temporal text length precedes detailed text
  parse; syntactic fraction length is guarded before derived fractional canonicality.
  For authored forms, intrinsic date/clock validity precedes exact expansion count,
  which is guarded before constructing any pieces.

  Clock amounts follow existing numeric bundle defaults and canonicality, with
  numeric-character checks before parse, authored exponent magnitude before
  normalization, then normalized coefficient/exponent limits. A rational descriptor
  is deferred/opaque after its containing history/shape is checked; malformed
  supported temporal input still fails before a deferred amount/domain error.

  The category [$ civilDateTime] specifically means the narrow profile literal
  grammar: offset/WTC/second-60 text in it is [$ invalid_operand] at stage 4, without
  rewriting the text. A recognized generic [$ temporal] category, civil-date tags,
  numeric/rational tags and existing deferred comparison categories have opaque
  payloads and receive stage-5 [$ unsupported_domain]. Unknown tags are malformed.
  The complete recognized vocabulary is [$ civilDateTime], [$ civilDateTimeSet],
  [$ civilDate], [$ civilDateSet], [$ finiteNumber], [$ exactNumber], [$ exactRational],
  [$ string], [$ boolean], [$ symbol], [$ positiveInfinity], [$ negativeInfinity], [$ nan],
  [$ explicitNull], [$ explicitAbsence], [$ toggle], [$ hex], [$ radix], [$ encoding],
  [$ separator], [$ sansaAddress], [$ referenceForm], [$ temporal], [$ container].
  Only the first two tags are supported under a temporal descriptor; only
  [$ finiteNumber] and [$ exactNumber] are supported under the amount's numerical
  descriptor. Other recognized tags are deferred. Validate their containing
  bundle and any supplied metadata, defaulting omitted deferred origins to
  undeclared and histories to none-declared, while leaving descriptor payload
  opaque. Schemas must enumerate this vocabulary; implementations cannot
  invent tags to opt out of required civil date-time cases.

  For amount guards, finite-text failures use [$ /input/amount/number/value].
  A derived combined coefficient/exponent text allowance uses
  [$ /input/amount/number]; normalized coefficient and exponent limits use their
  respective fields. For finite text, both normalized limits use its value field.
  Invalid boundary placement uses the point/start field, or the interval's
  [$ includeEnd] when an end marker is incorrectly included. Unknown metadata
  fields use the reached field's pointer under the containing bundle.

  Stage-6 local order:

  1. Calendar shift: work then intermediate-candidate guards, exact pointwise image
     with whole-result domain validation, canonical union. A reached work failure
     precedes a prospective domain error under this new date-time recipe.
  2. Clock shift: compact extreme/domain preflight; numeric-workspace guard, then
     generated fraction guard; work then intermediate guards; exact translation.
  3. Relations: summed input-piece work guard, then exact symbolic relation.
  4. Materialize: P + S work guard, then S candidate guard, then compare the one
     complete-form template. A mismatch becomes stage-7 [$ unrepresentable_result].
  5. Step: P + S work guard then S candidate guard, source-template equality
     ([$ incompatible_operand] on mismatch), target-period domain preflight, total
     P + S + T work and S + T candidate guards, then target construction.

  Operand/configuration paths identify the failing field. Input-piece allowance
  uses [$ .../temporal/pieces] for a derived set and [$ .../temporal] for an authored
  form's expansion. A text/fraction allowance uses its payload/coordinate field.
  Stage-6 step mismatch uses [$ /input/value/temporal]. Operation domain/workspace/
  work/candidate/final-output failures use null paths. Missing explicit profile
  uses the path in section 1. Whole-operation failures do not name an arbitrary
  first image candidate, whose sort/normalization can differ internally.

  Consequently a partial-month step with zero work allowance fails [$ resource_limit]
  before eligibility, unlike the frozen civil-date contract's preflight order.
  This source-matching precedence applies to this new contract and does not change
  its predecessor. The local order also
  makes a calendar shift exceeding both work and domain fail work first, while
  a clock shift's compact domain failure precedes workspace/work. These orders are normative and are exercised by competing-failure CTS cases.

  ## 10. Resource map and harness boundary [# civil-datetime-section-15]

  The following values are the explicit CTS baseline, not verified native defaults
  or machine capacity. CTS semantic vectors must supply complete
  explicit maps with adequate capacity; native defaults need independent probes.

  | Limit | CTS baseline | Counting scope |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ maxRequestBytes] | 65,536 | Actual logical JSON UTF-8, excluding outer CTS framing. |
  | [$ maxRequestDepth] | 32 | Per-path container depth, root object/array at one. |
  | [$ maxTemporalCharacters] | 1,024 | Each supplied literal/ordinary coordinate, before detailed parsing. |
  | [$ maxInputPieces] | 512 | Each temporal operand's full exact expansion or supplied array length. |
  | [$ maxIntermediatePieces] | 4,096 | Recipe candidates/reference pieces, cumulative per operation. |
  | [$ maxOutputPieces] | 512 | Final canonical extent, including compact materialized form expansion. |
  | [$ maxWork] | 16,384 | The agreed piece/day/reference work recipe. |
  | [$ maxFractionDigits] | 256 | Supplied fraction digits including trailing zeroes; canonical generated fraction digits. |
  | [$ maxCountCharacters] | 32 | Count text including sign, before conversion. |
  | [$ maxNumericCharacters] | 4,096 | Clock amount text, or coefficient plus exponent characters. |
  | [$ maxCoefficientDigits] | 1,024 | Normalized amount coefficient digits, excluding sign; zero costs one. |
  | [$ maxExponentMagnitude] | 1,000,000 | Authored and normalized amount exponent magnitude, even for zero. |
  | [$ maxWorkspaceDigits] | 4,096 | Expanded exact clock-shift workspace under section 8. |
  | [$ maxOutputCharacters] | 1,024 | Bare materialized date-time string, after exact representability. |
  | [$ maxResultBytes] | 131,072 | Complete successful compact logical JSON envelope before serialization. |
  |~ Civil date-time resource limits and conformance baseline

  Use saturating counts/length arithmetic to reject above a selected allowance
  before allocation. Request budgets cover opaque deferred payloads as well.
  Successful-envelope sizing uses compact JSON without optional whitespace; strings have bounded
  ASCII content in successes. No optional diagnostic message belongs in a success.
  The large result allowance intentionally permits ordinary reduced-year examples;
  it does not promise every combination of all maxima. Coordinate fractions and
  metadata still contribute their actual serialized bytes.

  Trusted limits are outside logical policy/context. They must be an object of
  known nonnegative safe integers; explicit null, arrays, scalars, unknown names,
  fractional limits and unsafe numeric values are harness configuration errors,
  never operation values or silent defaults. A default map can supply omitted
  known settings; conformance vectors use explicit complete maps. Resource failure
  cannot turn an error into a null, approximation or empty/partial set.

  Logical raw JSON decoding must preserve numeric token spelling, duplicate-key
  evidence and exact byte counts even inside opaque payloads, including [$ -0],
  overflowing exponents and underflowing magnitudes. Native structured bindings
  must not repeatedly expand shared subgraphs before admission; cycle/depth/size
  handling must be bounded and fail closed. Unsupported getters/proxies/subclasses
  belong in the documented host boundary, not semantic profile opt-outs.

  All limits are per operation. Applications manage whole-chain budgets. Required
  no-value diagnostics are not suppressed recursively by successful-output limits.
  No machine-specific timeout or private allocation optimization changes the
  portable admission rule or proves semantic conformance merely by rejecting work.

`
